INT. FLOATING LIBRARY - LANTERN HALL - TWILIGHT

Shelves rise in spirals into a sky of drifting clouds. Books hover on invisible currents, flapping their covers like moths. Lanterns bob at the ends of long gilt chains. Rain falls upward past the windows.

IMELDA OGUNLEYE (50s, patchwork robes, spectacles on a silver chain) sits at a vast oak desk, writing with a feather that dips itself into the ink. Before her stands BARTHOLOMEW QUILL (30s, travel-stained coat, a leather satchel), hat in hand. Perched on a high shelf, a small gray owl-shaped librarian called PIP watches them both with unblinking amber eyes.

IMELDA
Sit.

BARTHOLOMEW
I'd rather stand.

IMELDA
You'll sit, or the books won't speak to you.

He sits. A book drifts down and lands in his lap, spine trembling.

BARTHOLOMEW
Is it always like this?

IMELDA
Only on Tuesdays.

BARTHOLOMEW
It's Thursday.

IMELDA
Then it is worse than I thought.

PIP
(a soft hoot)
Hoo.

IMELDA
Pip says you are late.

BARTHOLOMEW
I came as soon as the letter reached me.

IMELDA
It reached you three weeks ago.

BARTHOLOMEW
The roads were closed.

IMELDA
The roads are always closed. That is what roads are for.

He opens the book. The pages are blank. He turns them. Blank. Blank. A single line of ink slowly blooms on the last page, in handwriting he recognizes.

BARTHOLOMEW
That's my mother's hand.

IMELDA
Yes.

BARTHOLOMEW
She wrote this?

IMELDA
She is writing it now.

BARTHOLOMEW
She's been dead for nine years.

IMELDA
In this library, no one is dead until the last page is read.

Silence. Rain falls up. A lantern knocks softly against a shelf.

BARTHOLOMEW
What does it say?

IMELDA
Read it aloud.

BARTHOLOMEW
"My dear boy. If you are reading this, I have kept my promise."

PIP
Hoo.

IMELDA
Go on.

BARTHOLOMEW
"I have kept it a very long time."

His voice cracks. Imelda takes off her spectacles and polishes them on her sleeve, looking elsewhere. Pip tucks his head under one wing.

BARTHOLOMEW (CONT'D)
Is there more?

IMELDA
There is always more.

BARTHOLOMEW
How long may I stay?

IMELDA
Until the lanterns burn down.

BARTHOLOMEW
And when is that?

IMELDA
When you stop reading.

He bends over the page. The lanterns brighten.
